{"term":"ore","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:32b5e9de2598c4059c","definition":"Rock or other material that contains valuable or utilitarian materials; primarily a rock containing metals or gems for which it is typically mined and processed.","example":"They general­ly occur in the earth in what are called veins, and are seldom found in the pure metallic state, but generally in combination with some other substance, in which state they are call­ed ores.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ore#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:8fc610583867ce9c03","definition":"A unit of currency used in England around the 10th to 12th centuries.","example":"In the time of Æthelred the Unready when the pound contained 240 pence, the ore was reckoned at 16 pence, but in earlier times there was probably much variation.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["ora"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ore#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:ad18cc1bacec619d34","definition":"Seaweed, especially that which is washed up ashore.","example":"","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"archaic, dialectal","register":"archaic, dialectal, uncountable","wordFamily":[],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ore#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:a797f081c1daa4a290","definition":"A type of fine wool, especially of the type historically produced in the market town of Leominster, Herefordshire.","example":"But then the ore of Lempſter / By Got is never a Sempſter; / Th